      "writtenOn": "2023-06-27T18:06:04Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Just realized this: since we call into `controlResultHandler` here, this code is called from a different thread that our object normally uses. It likely means that access to `mCookie` also needs to be serialized. Otherwise, for example, the effect of a call to `unregisterPort` could not be visible on the callback thread yet.\n\nThus, either we should put `mCookie` under protection of the same mutex as `mState`, or make it atomic.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 92,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 92,
        "endChar": 38
      },
      "revId": "d99e2574319a6c04fd50c7d42be936d9c75c00e8",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8cf82456_b5273558",
        "filename": "audio/aidl/default/bluetooth/DevicePortProxy.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 36
      },
      "lineNbr": 92,
      "author": {
        "id": 1150374
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-06-29T17:43:27Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I dont think this is correct. \n\nmCookie is modified during registerPort and unregisterPort. Every other occurrence is read-only. During registerPort, while mState is being set to STANDBY, mCvMutex is unlocked making the mCookie initialization also visible to all threads. Further, during unregisterPort, the first call is to UnregisterControlResultCback(). This will unregister callbacks. So visibility of mCookie to callbacks is no longer relevant as those callbacks methods cannot be reached.\n\nFurther more, calls to DevicePortProxy methods are made thread safe by mStreamLock in StreamBluetooth.cpp. Say DevicePortProxy##read(), DevicePortProxy##close() are called parallelly and say close acquires lock first, then as cookie is reset to invalid value, mutex unlocking will evict the cache lines making other threads to see the updated value.\n\nThe only concern is unregisterPort being called when callback are still in service. This is a possibility because, start()-\u003econdWaitState() is not a blocking call. After kMaxWaitingTimeMs, the start()/suspend() call returns. Say callback is stuck due to cpu thread overheating, start()/suspend() call came back, and then unregisterPort() is called, then it is the responsibility of BluetoothSessionControl to deal with it but not DevicePortProxy.",
      "parentUuid": "3eb6690c_adcfc13e",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 92,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 92,
        "endChar": 38
      },
      "revId": "d99e2574319a6c04fd50c7d42be936d9c75c00e8",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e30f03fc_faf05fee",
        "filename": "audio/aidl/default/bluetooth/DevicePortProxy.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 36
      },
      "lineNbr": 92,
      "author": {
        "id": 1042638
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-06-29T22:25:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e mCvMutex is unlocked making the mCookie initialization also visible to all threads.\n\nThat does not seem to be entirely correct. Citing again https://eel.is/c++draft/intro.races:\n\n\"Informally, performing a release operation on A forces prior side effects on other memory locations to become visible to other threads that later perform a consume or an acquire operation on A.\"\n\nSo, not to **all** threads, but only on those that acquire the same mutex, `mCvMutex` in our case. And only after they have acquired it, not immediately after the mutating thread has unlocked its mutex. Since in `controlResultHandler`, the `inUse` function which checks the cookie is called before acquiring `mCvMutex`, it is not guaranteed that it will actually see the update.",
      "parentUuid": "8cf82456_b5273558",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 92,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 92,
        "endChar": 38
      },
      "revId": "d99e2574319a6c04fd50c7d42be936d9c75c00e8",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
